Camouflage, compartmentation, coded language, and selective visibility are all real human tools. But the key epistemic trap is this:

hard to see ≠ proof it is there
and
hard to disprove ≠ evidence it is true

Good tradecraft and good reasoning actually share the same discipline: separate observation from inference, keep competing explanations alive, and look for something that could genuinely falsify your preferred story.

So the useful SEAL-style version is:

conceal when appropriate
observe broadly
assume the picture is incomplete
verify before committing
move when the evidence is sufficient

That keeps camouflage as a capability instead of letting it become a universal explanation for anything that feels hidden.

And yes—brotherhood can be a strong coordination layer when it is grounded in shared standards, not just shared suspicion.